---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/story.css';
---
<Base wide title="Matrix for Claude Code: a local index for your documents" description="A free plugin that gives Claude Code a local, searchable index of PDFs, Word files, scans and charts. Measured on 442 pages: 42 instead of 126 seconds per question, less than half the input tokens.">
<div class="story">

  <section class="beat" style="border-top:0">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Matrix for Claude Code</p>
      <h1>Index your documents once. Then ask Claude Code.</h1>
      <p class="big">A free plugin that lets Claude Code search your PDFs, Word files, scans and charts by meaning.
      Answers come with file, page and passage. The index lives on your Mac. Claude Code is the language model,
      so you need no extra model and no API key.</p>
      <a class="go" href="https://github.com/matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp">Get it on GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in split">
      <div><p class="kicker">The problem</p><h2>Without an index, every question starts from zero.</h2></div>
      <div>
        <p class="big">Claude Code converts the PDFs to text, greps, opens pages, greps again. That works. On a few
        hundred pages it takes 20 or more tool calls per question.</p>
        <p class="muted">Matrix reads the documents once. It splits them by their structure, indexes them and gives
        Claude Code short hits instead of whole pages.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">The measurement</p>
      <h2>Same answers in a third of the time.</h2>
      <p class="big">Claude Code alone against Claude Code with the plugin, on public EU regulations and reports.
      Same questions, same prompt. The right answers were written down before the first run.</p>
      <div class="numbers">
        <div><strong>42 s</strong><span>average time per question on 442 pages, instead of 126 s without the plugin</span></div>
        <div><strong>303k</strong><span>input tokens per question, instead of 651k</span></div>
        <div><strong>9.5</strong><span>tool calls per question, instead of 21</span></div>
        <div><strong>−34 %</strong><span>input tokens on 1,592 pages in 14 documents and four languages; 35 % less time</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="demo">
          <thead><tr><th>Question (442 pages)</th><th>Alone</th><th>With Matrix</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>English question, German legal text</td><td>86 s · 381k tokens</td><td class="ok">26 s · 187k tokens</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A number that exists only in a chart</td><td>109 s · 585k tokens</td><td class="ok">51 s · 407k tokens</td></tr>
            <tr><td>One answer spread over three regulations</td><td>245 s · 1,279k tokens</td><td class="ok">62 s · 428k tokens</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Paraphrased question, single regulation</td><td>64 s · 357k tokens</td><td class="ok">27 s · 191k tokens</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="muted" style="margin-top:1.5rem">Averages per run, Claude Code with Sonnet, October 2026. Questions, right answers
      and the script to rerun it are in <a href="https://github.com/matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp/blob/main/DEMO.md">DEMO.md</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">The limits</p>
      <h2>It saves effort. It doesn't make Claude smarter.</h2>
      <p class="big">Claude Code alone answered every question correctly too.</p>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>More documents, more gain</h3><p>The gain is largest when the question uses other words or another language than the text. For one short document it is small.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Spread-out questions gain less</h3><p>On the larger set, a question across three acts saved almost nothing: Claude still searches once per act.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Indexing costs minutes</h3><p>Once per folder: 16 minutes for 1,592 pages, no Claude tokens. Worth it if you ask more than once.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Page numbers slipped</h3><p>In the measured version, two citations were one page off where a passage crosses a page break. File, article and passage were right in every answer.</p></div>
        <div><h3>One miss, then fixed</h3><p>One answer was wrong because a chart label was too small to read. After a fix in the skill, 4 of 4 were right.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Small samples</h3><p>Two runs per question in the first test, one in the second. Run-to-run differences of 10–20 % are normal.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">How it works</p>
      <h2>Code finds the passages. Claude reads them.</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><div><h3>Split along the real structure <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>By PDF layout, headings, articles and tables. No language model decides where a section ends. Scans and charts are indexed too.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Search by meaning and by keyword <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>A question in other words or another language still finds the provision. Cross-references between provisions are followed. More on this below.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Short hits <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>About 1,160 tokens per search instead of 5,500. Each hit is an excerpt with file, pages and the path document › chapter › article.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Read before citing <span class="who ai">AI</span></h3><p>Claude Code opens the passage or the article it wants to quote. Then it answers with file, page and passage.</p></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Check the citations <span class="who rules">rules</span></h3><p>Each citation is checked against the original text.</p></div></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in">
      <p class="kicker">Vector search</p>
      <h2>Vector search without a vector database.</h2>
      <p class="big">Every passage gets a vector from a multilingual embedding model (EmbeddingGemma 2), computed on the
      Mac. The vectors are stored in the same SQLite file as the text.</p>
      <div class="grid3">
        <div><h3>A full scan is enough</h3><p>Matrix compares the question with every vector (cosine similarity). On 5,950 passages and 408 chart pages a search takes 56 ms. No index to tune, no extra server.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Three lists, one ranking</h3><p>Vector hits, keyword hits (SQLite full-text search, BM25) and hits on chart pages are merged by rank (reciprocal rank fusion). Keywords find article numbers, vectors find paraphrases.</p></div>
        <div><h3>A second model re-sorts</h3><p>An optional re-ranking model (bge-reranker-v2-m3) orders the top candidates. The right place was in the first five for 27 of 30 test questions instead of 20.</p></div>
        <div><h3>One vector space per collection</h3><p>Vectors from different models are never compared. Two models can produce vectors of the same length, so Matrix stores which model wrote each one.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Charts get vectors too</h3><p>Pages with graphics are embedded as images with the same model. A value that exists only in a chart is found on its page.</p></div>
        <div><h3>Fixed test questions</h3><p>30 questions with the right places written down in advance. Every change to the search is measured against them.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat">
    <div class="in split">
      <div><p class="kicker">Two lines</p><h2>Install it, then just ask.</h2></div>
      <div>
        <div class="card"><pre><code>claude plugin marketplace add matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp
claude plugin install matrix@matrix-mcp</code></pre></div>
        <p class="muted">Start Claude Code in the folder with your documents and ask. The first time, it walks you
        through the setup. Needs a Mac with Apple silicon and Node.js 18 or later.</p>
        <p class="muted">Matrix reads the folder you started in and asks before touching any other. It never changes or
        deletes your files. The only text that leaves your Mac is what Claude Code reads anyway.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="beat alt">
    <div class="in read">
      <p class="kicker">Where it comes from</p>
      <h2>It started as an app.</h2>
      <p class="big">The plugin runs on the same Rust core as <a href="/projects/matrix/">Matrix</a>, my document app for Mac
      and iPhone. In the app a local model does the reading; here Claude Code does.</p>
      <a class="go" href="https://github.com/matthiasnordwig/matrix-mcp">Get it on GitHub</a>
      <a class="go ghost" href="mailto:matthias.nordwig@programmiere.de?subject=Matrix%20for%20Claude%20Code">Write to me</a>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>
</Base>
